     1	# Design specification: rewrite of the biface morphometrics Shiny app
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-11-17
     4	Branch: `app-rewrite`
     5	Replaces: `bifaceAnalysis/app.R` at commit f8eeed2 (the version deployed at
     6	https://lipolab.shinyapps.io/bifaceAnalysis/)
     7	Status: design, not yet built. To be audited before implementation.
     8	
     9	## 1. Purpose and audience
    10	
    11	The app teaches outline-based shape analysis of bifaces and projectile points to
    12	student analysts. A student photographs or uploads artifact images, extracts
    13	outlines, and examines shape variation in the assemblage. The rewrite has three
    14	goals set by the author: make the app more robust, make it simpler to use, and
    15	give students more information about what the results mean.
    16	
    17	## 2. Defects in the current app that the design must remove
    18	
    19	Each item was confirmed by reading `bifaceAnalysis/app.R` at f8eeed2. Line
    20	numbers refer to that file.
    21	
    22	Results that are wrong:
    23	
    24	- D1. Harmonic power (lines 1562 to 1575) groups coefficient columns as
    25	  `(4*(h-1)+1):(4*h)`. Momocs orders the columns A1..An, B1..Bn, C1..Cn, D1..Dn,
    26	  so each bar sums coefficients from unrelated harmonics.
    27	- D2. Region variance, variance heatmap, region correlations, and PC1
    28	  contributions (lines 1668 to 1947) compare outline point i across specimens.
    29	  Points are produced by `coo_interpolate(n = 100)` from a contour whose start
    30	  point is arbitrary (line 788), so point i is not the same location on
    31	  different specimens. `custom_mshape` averages non-corresponding points.
    32	- D3. The "modularity index" (lines 1903 to 1925) is described as the covariance
    33	  ratio of Adams (2016) [CITE-CHECK: Adams 2016]. It is a sum of correlations
    34	  divided by a sum of mean distances and is not that statistic.
    35	- D4. Geometric region detection applies curvature from the last processed
    36	  specimen to the mean shape (lines 1239 to 1251). The left blade index range is
    37	  computed with the same expression as the right blade (lines 279 to 292).
    38	  `find_inflection_points` (lines 90 to 112) recurses without a stopping rule.
    39	- D5. Manual mode collects blade-junction points that are never used, and applies
    40	  point indices chosen on one specimen to the mean shape (lines 1125 to 1233).
    41	- D6. The "Thin-Plate Splines" tab computes no thin-plate spline (lines 1632 to
    42	  1665).
    43	
    44	Robustness defects:
    45	
    46	- D7. Each press of "Process Image" appends an outline (lines 803 to 808).
    47	  Reprocessing an image duplicates the specimen. No outline can be removed.
    48	- D8. Defaults ("darker than background", tip "up") do not match the demo
    49	  images, which show light artifacts on a dark background with the tip to the
    50	  left.
    51	- D9. The largest contour is chosen by point count, not area (lines 766 to 777).
    52	  Ink catalog numbers on the demo artifacts produce holes in the mask.
    53	- D10. Click markers use image coordinates as CSS pixels (lines 590 to 606).
    54	- D11. Analysis fails with one specimen, with fewer than three specimens, and
    55	  when the cluster count exceeds the specimen count.
    56	- D12. `efourier(norm = TRUE)` after `coo_align()` (lines 1077 to 1085) does not
    57	  fix which end is the tip, so near-symmetric outlines can be rotated 180
    58	  degrees relative to each other.
    59	- D13. Momocs was archived from CRAN on 2026-03-18. A redeploy cannot install it
    60	  from CRAN.
    61	- D14. The PDF handler duplicates about 300 lines of plotting code. The README
    62	  describes exports and a script that the app does not contain.
    63	
    64	## 3. Decisions already made by the author (2026-11-17)
    65	
    66	1. Elliptical Fourier analysis is implemented in base R inside the app and
    67	   validated numerically against Momocs 1.4.1. Momocs is not a runtime
    68	   dependency.
    69	2. Region analysis is rebuilt on corresponding points. The invented modularity
    70	   index is dropped. The covariance ratio is not computed.
    71	3. Work is done on branch `app-rewrite` of `clipo/bifaceAnalysis`. Nothing is
    72	   pushed or deployed without the author's instruction.
    73	4. Additions: interpretation text beside each result, scaled linear
    74	   measurements, group labels, and data exports.
    75	
    76	## 4. Scope
    77	
    78	In scope: the Shiny app in `bifaceAnalysis/`, its tests, and its README.
    79	
    80	Out of scope: the command-line script `biface_analysis.R` and the PDF and CSV
    81	outputs at the repository root. That script shares defects D1, D2, and D12. The
    82	README will state this. The script is not modified in this work.
    83	
    84	Not included, with reasons: per-specimen shoulder landmarks (adds two clicks per
    85	specimen and does not apply to lanceolate forms), the covariance ratio (author
    86	decision 2), and thin-plate spline deformation grids (PC shape reconstructions
    87	in section 7.4 serve the teaching purpose more directly).
    88	
    89	## 5. Dependencies and layout
    90	
    91	Runtime packages: shiny, bslib, imager, ggplot2. imager 1.0.8 is current on CRAN
    92	(published 2025-12-23). dplyr and Momocs are removed.
    93	
    94	```
    95	bifaceAnalysis/            deployed app directory
    96	  app.R                    UI and server wiring only
    97	  R/image.R                thresholding, mask cleaning, contour extraction
    98	  R/outline.R              orientation, landmarks, resampling, measurements
    99	  R/efa.R                  elliptical Fourier forward, inverse, power
   100	  R/shape.R                Procrustes alignment, PCA, clustering, regions
   101	  R/plots.R                one plotting function per figure (screen and PDF)
   102	  R/interpret.R            interpretation text built from computed values
   103	  www/                     demo images
   104	tests/testthat/            unit tests (outside the deployed directory)
   105	tests/fixtures/            Momocs 1.4.1 reference values and generating script
   106	docs/specs/, docs/audits/
   107	```
   108	
   109	Tests and the Momocs fixture script sit outside `bifaceAnalysis/` so that
   110	rsconnect's dependency scan does not record testthat or Momocs for deployment.
   111	
   112	## 6. Processing pipeline
   113	
   114	All functions in this section are pure functions of their inputs, with no Shiny
   115	dependency, so each can be unit tested.
   116	
   117	### 6.1 Image to binary mask
   118	
   119	1. Load JPEG or PNG. If the longer side exceeds 1200 pixels, downscale to 1200.
   120	   All later coordinates, including scale calibration, are in the downscaled
   121	   image.
   122	2. Optional crop from a drag rectangle (Shiny brush). Default is the whole image.
   123	3. Convert to grayscale. Compute a global threshold by Otsu's method on a
   124	   256-bin histogram (implemented in base R). The student can override the
   125	   threshold with a slider.
   126	4. Polarity. The artifact is assumed not to touch the crop border. The class
   127	   (above or below threshold) holding the majority of border pixels is
   128	   background. The student can override.
   129	5. Morphological opening with a student-set radius (default 2 pixels) removes
   130	   small specks.
   131	6. Fill holes: every background component that does not touch the border
   132	   becomes foreground. This removes ink labels and specular highlights.
   133	7. Keep the connected component with the largest pixel area.
   134	8. Extract the single closed boundary of that component.
   135	
   136	Quality checks, shown as plain warnings and never as silent failures: the mask
   137	touches the crop border; the mask covers less than 1 percent or more than 90
   138	percent of the crop; the boundary has fewer than 100 points; no foreground
   139	found.
   140	
   141	### 6.2 Orientation and landmarks
   142	
   143	Coordinates are converted to a y-up system. Traversal direction is set to
   144	counterclockwise using the sign of the shoelace area.
   145	
   146	1. The student declares the tip direction for the image (up, down, left, right).
   147	   The setting carries over to the next image.
   148	2. Compute the two principal axes of the outline polygon from its area second
   149	   moments. Choose the axis whose direction is within 45 degrees of the declared
   150	   tip direction. This is the long axis for most points and the short axis for
   151	   points that are wider than long. Orient the unit vector u toward the declared
   152	   tip direction.
   153	3. If the ratio of the principal second moments is below 1.1, the axes are
   154	   poorly determined. Use the declared direction itself as u and show a warning.
   155	4. Tip landmark T: the outline point with the largest projection on u.
   156	5. Midline: the line through the area centroid parallel to u. Base landmark B:
   157	   the intersection of the midline with the outline that is most distant from T
   158	   in the direction of -u.
   159	6. Manual override: the student can click T and B on the outline preview. u then
   160	   runs from B to T.
   161	7. Rotate so that u points up.
   162	
   163	### 6.3 Corresponding points
   164	
   165	T and B divide the outline into two edges. Each edge is resampled by linear
   166	interpolation to 100 points equally spaced in arc length, starting at T for the
   167	first edge and at B for the second, giving 200 points with T at index 1 and B at
   168	index 101. Point k therefore lies at the same relative arc-length position
   169	between tip and base on every specimen.
   170	
   171	Stated limitation (shown in the app): this correspondence is geometric. Equal
   172	arc-length fractions are not guaranteed to fall on the same morphological
   173	feature. A point with long barbs has more edge length in the shoulder zone than
   174	a lanceolate point. Which face was photographed is arbitrary, so left and right
   175	are not comparable across specimens, and all region results pool both edges.
   176	
   177	### 6.4 Measurements
   178	
   179	Computed from the oriented full-resolution outline, in pixels and in millimetres
   180	when a scale is set: length (extent along u), maximum width (extent
   181	perpendicular to u), length/width, position of maximum width as a fraction of
   182	length from the base, area (shoelace), perimeter, and an asymmetry score. The
   183	asymmetry score is the Procrustes distance between the 200-point configuration
   184	and its mirror image about the tip-base axis with the two edges relabelled.
   185	
   186	Scale calibration: the student clicks two points on the scale bar and enters the
   187	distance in millimetres. Calibration is stored per image. A checkbox applies the
   188	current calibration to all images, for batches photographed at a fixed distance.
   189	Without calibration, only pixel values and ratios are reported and the app says
   190	so.
   191	
   192	### 6.5 Specimen store
   193	
   194	Specimens are keyed by image name. Saving an outline for an image that already
   195	has one replaces it. Each specimen holds: name (editable), group label
   196	(editable, optional), the 200 corresponding points in pixel units, the
   197	full-resolution oriented outline, measurements, calibration, and the processing
   198	settings used. Specimens can be removed. The store can be downloaded as a CSV of
   199	outline coordinates and reloaded later, because shinyapps.io sessions time out.
   200	CSV is used instead of RDS because `readRDS` on an uploaded file is unsafe.
   201	
   202	## 7. Analysis
   203	
   204	Minimum sample sizes are enforced with a plain message, not an error: 2
   205	specimens for the outline overlays, 3 for PCA, 4 for clustering and region
   206	covariation.
   207	
   208	### 7.1 Alignment
   209	
   210	Generalized Procrustes alignment of the 200-point configurations: translate to
   211	the centroid, scale to unit centroid size, rotate (no reflection) to the
   212	iteratively updated mean by least squares until the change in summed squared
   213	distance is below 1e-10. The mean shape is rotated so its tip-base axis is
   214	vertical. Implemented in base R. Points are treated as fixed, not sliding,
   215	semilandmarks.
   216	
   217	An optional setting analyses symmetrized shapes (the average of each
   218	configuration and its relabelled mirror image). Default is off. The app explains
   219	that with the photographed face arbitrary, asymmetric variation has an arbitrary
   220	sign.
   221	
   222	### 7.2 Elliptical Fourier analysis
   223	
   224	Coefficients a_n, b_n, c_n, d_n for n = 1..H are computed from the aligned closed
   225	200-point polygons with the Kuhl and Giardina (1982) formulas
   226	[CITE-CHECK: Kuhl and Giardina 1982; the PDF is not in the repository]. With
   227	segment increments dx_p, dy_p, dt_p = sqrt(dx_p^2 + dy_p^2), cumulative length
   228	t_p, and perimeter T:
   229	
   230	    a_n = T / (2 n^2 pi^2) * sum_p (dx_p/dt_p) [cos(2 n pi t_p / T) - cos(2 n pi t_{p-1} / T)]
   231	    b_n = T / (2 n^2 pi^2) * sum_p (dx_p/dt_p) [sin(2 n pi t_p / T) - sin(2 n pi t_{p-1} / T)]
   232	
   233	c_n and d_n use dy_p in place of dx_p.
   234	
   235	No first-ellipse normalization is applied. Size, position, rotation, and start
   236	point are already standardized by sections 6.2, 6.3, and 7.1. This removes
   237	defect D12.
   238	
   239	Harmonic power for harmonic n is (a_n^2 + b_n^2 + c_n^2 + d_n^2) / 2, averaged
   240	over specimens. Cumulative power is reported both including and excluding the
   241	first harmonic, labelled as such. The default H is the smallest number of
   242	harmonics reaching 99 percent of cumulative power excluding the first harmonic,
   243	bounded to 6..30. The student can override H. A reconstruction panel shows one
   244	selected specimen rebuilt with 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and H harmonics over its original
   245	outline.
   246	
   247	Validation: (i) forward coefficients match `Momocs::efourier(norm = FALSE)` on
   248	the same coordinates to 1e-8, from a committed fixture; (ii) inverse of forward
   249	reproduces the input polygon within a stated tolerance at H = 100; (iii) an
   250	ellipse with semi-axes 3 and 1 sampled at 2000 points gives first-harmonic
   251	amplitudes 3 and 1 and higher harmonics below 1e-4; (iv) power computed by the
   252	app equals the same formula applied to the Momocs coefficient matrix with its
   253	documented column order.
   254	
   255	### 7.3 PCA and stability
   256	
   257	`prcomp` on the specimen-by-4H coefficient matrix, centered and unscaled.
   258	Reported: variance explained per component and a scree plot. Stability is
   259	described without inference: each specimen is left out in turn, the PCA is
   260	refitted, and the absolute cosine between the refitted and full-data loading
   261	vector is recorded for PC1 and PC2. The minimum and median are shown, with text
   262	explaining that values near 1 mean the axis does not depend on any single
   263	specimen.
   264	
   265	### 7.4 Shape change along components
   266	
   267	For PC1 to PC3, the mean coefficient vector plus and minus 2 standard deviations
   268	of the scores along the component is passed through the inverse transform and
   269	drawn over the mean shape. The morphospace plot shows PC scores with
   270	reconstructed shapes on a background grid, coloured by group label or cluster.
   271	This replaces the tab named "Thin-Plate Splines".
   272	
   273	### 7.5 Clustering
   274	
   275	Ward (D2) hierarchical clustering on Euclidean distances between coefficient
   276	vectors. The dendrogram is shown. The student sets k between 2 and n - 1. Mean
   277	silhouette width is shown for each k in that range as a descriptive guide. When
   278	group labels exist, a cluster-by-group table is shown. No test statistic or
   279	p-value is reported.
   280	
   281	### 7.6 Regions
   282	
   283	Regions are intervals of relative arc-length position from tip (0) to base (1),
   284	applied to both edges: tip, blade, shoulder, base, with default boundaries 0.15,
   285	0.60, 0.80, adjustable by the student. Membership is by point index and is
   286	identical on every specimen. Boundaries are drawn on the mean shape and on each
   287	specimen so the student can see where they fall.
   288	
   289	Outputs:
   290	
   291	- Per-point variance: the sum of the x and y variances of the Procrustes
   292	  coordinates at each point, drawn as colour on the mean shape.
   293	- Per-region variance: the mean per-point variance within the region.
   294	- Between-region covariation: the RV coefficient (Escoufier 1973)
   295	  [CITE-CHECK: Escoufier 1973] between the coordinate blocks of each pair of
   296	  regions. For each pair, the specimens of one block are permuted 999 times at a
   297	  fixed seed. The mean and the 2.5 to 97.5 percent range of the permuted RV are
   298	  drawn next to the observed value as a reference for what RV looks like at this
   299	  sample size with no association. No p-value is reported.
   300	
   301	Reason a frequentist reference is used here: a Bayesian model of covariance
   302	between coordinate blocks of 30 to 180 variables with fewer than 30 specimens
   303	is not tractable in an interactive classroom app, and the quantity is
   304	descriptive.
   305	
   306	Stated limitations (shown in the app): RV is biased upward when specimens are
   307	few relative to variables, which the permutation reference makes visible. A
   308	single Procrustes alignment of the whole outline spreads variation across
   309	regions and can induce covariation between them
   310	[CITE-CHECK: source for superimposition-induced covariance]. Region boundaries
   311	are proportional, not anatomical.
   312	
   313	## 8. Interface
   314	
   315	Four steps in a navigation bar, plus a Help page.
   316	
   317	1. Images. Choose demo images or upload JPEG or PNG files. A "Run demo" button
   318	   processes the four demo images with automatic settings and opens Results.
   319	2. Outline. One image at a time, with previous and next controls. The image,
   320	   mask, and oriented outline with T, B, and region boundaries update when any
   321	   setting changes; there is no separate "process" button. Controls: drag to
   322	   crop, threshold (automatic by default), polarity (automatic by default),
   323	   speck removal, tip direction, optional scale calibration, specimen name,
   324	   group label. Warnings from section 6.1 appear here. "Save outline" adds or
   325	   replaces the specimen.
   326	3. Specimens. A table of saved specimens with thumbnails of the outlines,
   327	   measurements, and group labels. Edit name or group, remove a specimen,
   328	   download or reload the outline CSV.
   329	4. Results. Sub-tabs: Outlines, Measurements, Harmonics, PCA, Shape change,
   330	   Clusters, Regions. Results recompute when specimens or settings change; there
   331	   is no "Run analysis" button. Each sub-tab has a "How to read this" panel
   332	   whose text is generated from the current values (section 9).
   333	
   334	Downloads: PDF report (figures, interpretation text, settings, and package
   335	versions), and CSV files for measurements, PC scores, Fourier coefficients, and
   336	outline coordinates.
   337	
   338	Figures use ggplot2, the Okabe-Ito palette for categories and viridis for
   339	continuous values, a sans-serif font, and no in-figure titles. The same plotting
   340	function produces the screen figure and the PDF page.
   341	
   342	## 9. Interpretation text
   343	
   344	`R/interpret.R` returns text built from computed values. Each statement reports
   345	a value and what it measures. Text does not make archaeological claims (function,
   346	chronology, cultural affiliation) and does not use significance language.
   347	Examples of content: the share of variance on PC1 and PC2 and the specimens at
   348	each extreme; what shape difference the PC1 reconstructions show, described by
   349	measured differences in length/width and position of maximum width between the
   350	plus and minus shapes; which region has the highest per-point variance and by
   351	what factor; the observed RV next to its permutation range; the leave-one-out
   352	stability values; and a statement of sample size with the number of components
   353	that can exist (n - 1).
   354	
   355	## 10. Testing and acceptance
   356	
   357	Unit tests (testthat), run from the repository root:
   358	
   359	- Otsu threshold on a synthetic bimodal image; polarity detection; hole filling;
   360	  largest-component selection with a second smaller object present.
   361	- Segmentation of the four demo images yields one closed outline each with no
