@@ -0,0 +1,216 @@
+import json
+from pathlib import Path
+
+stem = '2026-11-17_spec'
+spec = 'docs/specs/2026-11-17_app_rewrite_spec.md'
+findings = []
+def add(severity, classification, loc, claim, problem, evidence, repair, check):
+    findings.append(dict(id=f'F{len(findings)+1}', severity=severity, classification=classification,
+                         location=f'{spec}:{loc}', claim=claim, problem=problem, evidence=evidence,
+                         repair=repair, acceptance_check=check, supersedes=''))
+
+add('blocking','wrong','126-134; 317-318; 361-362',
+    'Automatic settings produce one closed artifact outline without warnings for every demo.',
+    'The border-majority rule reverses polarity for artifacts 1, 2 and 4. Hole filling then absorbs the light artifact into the selected dark-background component. These results touch the border and produce open contours.',
+    'Section 6.1 calls the majority border class background. checks.log records light-border fractions 0.525119617225, 0.520742358079, 0.310846560847 and 0.539735099338. Images 1, 2 and 4 each produce two open contours. Image 3 produces one closed 725-vertex contour. All checks used the original full images, Otsu, disk opening radius 2 and 4-connectivity.',
+    'Replace or qualify border-majority polarity with a rule that handles the light image margins and top scale bar. Specify connectivity, border handling and the opening kernel. Retain manual override and reject unusable masks before analysis.',
+    'Run the final specified pipeline on all four unchanged JPEGs. Assert one closed artifact contour, no border contact, no scale bar, filled enclosed ink holes, and the stated area/point-count checks. Save reference masks so closure alone cannot certify the background.')
+
+add('blocking','wrong','247-253',
+    'An ellipse with semi-axes 3 and 1 has first-harmonic amplitudes 3 and 1 and all higher harmonics below 1e-4 under the displayed formulas.',
+    'Those are angular-parameter Fourier expectations. The specified transform parameterizes the outline by arc length, so a noncircular ellipse has higher harmonics. Correct code cannot pass this test.',
+    'Independent R evaluation at 2000 vertices gives first-harmonic x/y amplitudes 2.62452424251 and 1.09927297350, with a third-harmonic coefficient 0.241790947734. Maximum coefficient disagreement with installed Momocs 1.4.1 is 3.47e-18 (checks.log).',
+    'Retain the arc-length equations. Replace the ellipse expectation with verified arc-length fixture values, and use a circle for the first-harmonic-only test. Keep reconstruction accuracy as a separate test.',
+    'The independent implementation and Momocs agree within 1e-8 on the same polygon and phase convention; the revised ellipse and circle fixtures both pass.')
+
+add('major','unsupported','224-253; 267-270',
+    'The specified coefficients suffice for reproducible inverse reconstruction, Momocs matching and automatic harmonic selection.',
+    'The inverse, constant terms, phase origin, evaluation positions and reconstruction error metric are absent. Landmark-centering does not force the arc-length mean to zero. Momocs starts its first segment at the last vertex, whereas the spec anchors index 1 at T. The denominator for the 99 percent power rule and the zero-higher-power case are also undefined.',
+    'The text gives only n=1..H equations and asks for an inverse within a stated tolerance without stating one. Installed efourier.default uses Dx <- coo[,1] - coo[,1][c(nr,1:(nr-1))]. A centered triangle has nonzero Fourier DC (0.0274850704906, 0.0393039712659), recorded in checks.log.',
+    'Specify x(t)=x0+sum(a_n cos+b_n sin), the analogous y(t), the arc-length constant terms or an explicit recentering convention, t=0 and polygon closure. Reconcile the Momocs fixture phase explicitly. Define evaluation times, error units/tolerance and which original/aligned outline is overlaid. Select H against a fixed declared reference spectrum and handle zero residual power.',
+    'An asymmetric polygon passes coefficient matching after the documented phase conversion and reconstructs in the declared frame. A circle, a sharp polygon, H below 16, and a zero-residual-power spectrum have deterministic results.')
+
+add('major','unsupported','148-161; 363-367',
+    'The orientation and landmark procedure determines T, B and u for every outline.',
+    'At exactly 45 degrees both principal axes qualify if the bound is inclusive, and neither qualifies if strict. A flat or broken tip can maximize projection at multiple points or along a whole segment. The optional click override has no snapping or invalid-landmark rules. A geometric extreme is not necessarily the surviving anatomical tip.',
+    'Step 2 says choose the axis within 45 degrees; step 4 says the outline point with the largest projection. Neither specifies ties. Step 6 permits clicking T and B but does not require distinct boundary points or define segment insertion.',
+    'Specify axis and tip tie handling, a warning/manual-resolution policy for broken or blunt ends, click projection onto segments, and rejection of coincident T/B. Define how B is inserted into the polygon. Keep the farthest-intersection rule if the authors accept its geometric meaning on concave bases.',
+    'Fixtures cover exact 45 degrees, a flat tip, truncated oblique tip, near-circle, wider-than-long outline, basal notch and a midline with several crossings. Expected landmarks or required manual intervention are explicit and invariant to contour start index.')
+
+add('major','unsupported','165-175; 283-287; 368',
+    'Two 100-point edge samples yield 200 distinct coordinates with T at 1 and B at 101.',
+    'Endpoint inclusion is unspecified. The usual inclusive sampling gives 200 rows but only 198 distinct points, duplicates B at 100/101 and T at 1/200, and creates zero-length segments in EFA. Region fractions must reverse on the second edge, but the index-to-fraction map and boundary ownership are absent.',
+    'Section 6.3 says 100 points starting at each landmark without specifying the excluded endpoint. checks.log reproduces 200 rows and 198 distinct points for inclusive sampling. Section 7.6 defines both edges from tip 0 to base 1 although the second stored edge starts at B.',
+    'Sample each directed edge at j/100 for j=0..99, excluding its destination. Specify second-edge tip fractions as 1-j/100, the mirror relabelling, and nonoverlapping interval endpoints. Define spacing as distance along the source contour, not Euclidean chords across corners.',
+    'A simple asymmetric polygon has 200 distinct vertices, T only at 1, B only at 101, all positive segment lengths, correct paired-edge fractions, and exactly one region assignment per point.')
+
+add('major','wrong','204-206; 257-270; 352-353',
+    'Same-number leave-one-out PC cosines describe stability, and PC1 to PC3 reconstructions are available once PCA is enabled at n=3.',
+    'Near-tied eigenvectors can exchange order or rotate within an unchanged subspace. The cosine is a valid ordered-vector sensitivity measure but cannot distinguish that from loss of the represented shape variation. At n=3, leave-one-out fits have rank at most 1, and the full fit has rank at most 2. Degenerate specimens can reduce rank further.',
+    'For rows (1.01,0), (-1.01,0), (0,1), (0,-1), omitting the first row gives PC1/PC2 cosines 0/0 while both subspace principal-angle cosines equal 1. checks.log also records ranks 2 and 1 before/after leaving one of three specimens out.',
+    'Use singular values of V_full^T V_refit to report principal angles for a declared leading eigenspace, with a rule for clusters of near-tied eigenvalues. Report individual-axis cosines only where identifiable. Freeze H and state whether GPA is refitted. Gate reconstructions and comparisons by numerical rank, and describe n-1 as an upper bound.',
+    'The order-swap example reports stable subspace and ambiguous axes. Three specimens do not produce PC3 or a rank-2 leave-one-out stability claim. Identical and rank-deficient shapes yield explicit unavailable messages without arbitrary zero-eigenvalue axes.')
+
+add('major','unsupported','119-121; 179-190; 347-350',
+    'Maximum width has a defined longitudinal position, and calibration can be applied to all images after independent downscaling.',
+    'The transverse bounding extent can use extrema at different heights, so it has no unique position along length. Rectangles also have tied maximum-width positions. A shared millimetres-per-downscaled-pixel factor is wrong when images have different resize factors. Full-resolution outline is ambiguous after mandatory downscaling.',
+    'Width is defined as extent perpendicular to u, followed by position of maximum width. All coordinates are declared downscaled in 6.1, while 6.4 requests a full-resolution outline and a checkbox applying current calibration to all images.',
+    'Distinguish transverse extent from maximum cross-sectional width. Define cross-sections, multiple intervals at notches, tied positions, and whether base means B or minimum axial extent. Store original dimensions and resize/crop transforms. Transfer scale through original-pixel units, or prohibit batch transfer across unequal resize factors. State area units, dimensionless outputs and the meaning of full-resolution.',
+    'Known rectangle, ellipse, slanted/asymmetric and notched fixtures yield declared widths/positions. Images with resize factors 1 and 0.5 give the same physical dimensions before/after crop and after batch calibration.')
+
+add('major','unsupported','194-200; 210-220; 283-287; 319-336',
+    'The specimen CSV restores the store, and setting changes reproducibly update analysis while preserving saved specimens.',
+    'A coordinate-only CSV does not define restoration of calibration, names/groups, full contours, landmarks or processing settings. Editable names and duplicate upload basenames leave identity unresolved. The spec does not say whether changing controls mutates saved outlines, whether symmetrization occurs before or after GPA and renormalization, or which measurements remain raw. Averaging with a mirror can change centroid size.',
